course: medicine
lesson: 32
title: Needles and brave faces
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Lots of children and plenty of grown-ups are scared of needles, and for most of them the worry beforehand is much worse than the quick sharp scratch itself.
explain: Fear sends your body into alarm mode: fast heart, tight muscles, held breath, and a tense body feels pain more, so slow breathing out really does make a jab hurt less.
explain: Being brave doesn't mean not being scared or not crying; it means feeling scared and doing the hard thing anyway, so a boy who cries through his jab and still holds out his arm is brave.
explain: Nurses have a whole kit of tricks for this: numbing cream or cold spray, sitting on a parent's lap, blowing bubbles, a video, a hand to squeeze, and an honest answer to every question.
fact: Fear of needles is very common in both children and adults, and it isn't babyish.
fact: For most injections the pain is a quick sharp scratch lasting a few seconds, and friends' stories are often exaggerated.
fact: Numbing cream or cold spray, sitting on a parent's lap and distraction all make needles easier, and children can ask for them.
fact: Distraction, such as counting, looking at a picture, blowing bubbles or squeezing a hand, helps the brain pay less attention to pain, and looking away is not cheating.
fact: Slow breathing, for example in for four and out for six, calms the body's fear response, while holding your breath tight can make it worse.
fact: Some people faint at the sight of needles or blood because their blood pressure drops suddenly, and if you've fainted before you can ask to lie down.
fact: Crying is a natural way to show fear or pain and let out stress, and dentists and nurses see it every day.
fact: Telling a child «be a big boy» is meant to help, but it can make children feel they mustn't show feelings; boys can cry too.
fact: Knowing what will happen reduces fear, so children are allowed to ask what, why and how long before a treatment.
fact: Stickers and praise help children remember the good part of a hard experience, and they deserve praise even if they were scared.
fact: Little children screaming at jabs aren't being naughty, and a calm older brother or sister holding their hand can really help.
exercise: Is being scared of needles babyish? | No, it's very common in children and adults | Yes, only babies and toddlers are ever scared of them | Yes, grown-ups never are | Only if you cry or shout while it happens
exercise: What does most injections' pain feel like? | A quick sharp scratch for a few seconds | Hours of terrible throbbing pain afterwards | Nothing at all, ever | A burning feeling that lasts for days
exercise: Is the worry before a jab usually worse than the jab? | Yes, for most people | No, the jab is always much worse | They're exactly the same, no difference | Nobody ever worries before a jab
exercise: What can make the needle hurt less? | Numbing cream or cold spray | Holding your breath really tight | Watching the needle closely the whole time | Tensing every muscle in your arm
exercise: Is looking away during a jab cheating? | No, distraction helps the brain feel less pain | Yes, you must watch or it doesn't count | Yes, the nurse needs you to look so she can see | Only for babies
exercise: Which breathing helps before a jab? | Slow breaths, longer out than in | Holding your breath tight until it's over | Panting fast like a dog after a run | Not breathing at all until it's done
exercise: Why do some people faint at blood tests? | Their blood pressure drops suddenly from fear or the sight of blood | They lose too much blood | The needle is poisonous | They are weak people who don't eat properly or ever exercise enough
exercise: What can you do if you've fainted before at a blood test? | Tell the nurse and ask to lie down | Hide it from the nurse so she isn't worried | Stand up straight and tense your legs | Skip breakfast and lunch
exercise: What does being brave mean? | Doing the hard thing even when scared | Never feeling scared of anything at all | Never crying or making a fuss, whatever happens | Laughing loudly at the needle to scare it
exercise: Can boys cry when they're hurt or scared? | Yes, crying is natural for everyone | No, only girls are allowed to cry | No, boys must hide it from everyone | Only babies cry
exercise: Why might "be a big boy" not help? | It can make a child feel he mustn't show feelings | It always helps a child be braver, every single time | It makes the needle look smaller and less scary | It's rude to nurses
exercise: Why does asking questions before treatment help? | Knowing what happens reduces fear | It delays the jab forever if you keep going | Doctors give a prize for good questions | It makes the doctor cross
exercise: Why do nurses give stickers? | To help you remember the good part and praise you | Because you must pay the nurse with them | To mark which arm had the jab, so it isn't done twice | To show you were naughty
exercise: How can a big sister help a screaming toddler at jabs? | Hold her hand and talk calmly | Tell her she's naughty and must stop it now | Leave the room until it's all over | Scream too
test: Mate: "injections are literally the worst pain on Earth". Truth? | Most are a quick scratch, stories get exaggerated | He's right, nothing on Earth hurts more than an injection | They hurt for a whole week | They can break bones
test: You cried at the dentist and feel embarrassed. Best thought? | Crying is normal, and I still did it | I'm a baby now and everyone knows it | Dentists hate criers | I should never go back to the dentist again
test: Dad says "big boys don't cry" before stitches. What could you say? | It helps me to hold your hand and cry if I need | Nothing, he's right about that | I promise I'll never cry again, Dad | Stitches don't hurt anyone, so it's fine
test: "count backwards from 20 while she does it, it works" — why? | Distraction makes the brain pay less attention to pain | Counting numbs the arm | The nurse can only work while you are counting out loud | It's magic
test: What would happen if a nurse just jabbed a scared kid with no warning or explanation? | The child would likely be more scared next time | The child would never be scared of needles ever again | Nothing different | The jab would hurt a lot less
test: Zoe fainted last time. What's the smart plan this time? | Tell the nurse and have it lying down | Stand up tall and stare hard at the needle | Not eat or drink anything all day | Run out of the room before it starts
test: In a cartoon the hero laughs at a giant needle to look tough. What's real bravery? | Feeling scared and doing it anyway | Never feeling scared of anything | Laughing at nurses and doctors | Hiding feelings
test: Toddler screams at jabs. Is she being naughty? | No, little children scream when scared or hurt | Yes, she should be told off firmly | Yes, she's spoiled and wants attention | No, she's just pretending to be hurt
test: You tense every muscle and hold your breath for the jab. Good idea? | No, slow breathing and a loose arm usually hurts less | Yes, it blocks pain | Yes, a tight muscle makes the needle bounce off | It makes no difference
test: Nurse asks if you want numbing cream. You think it's for wimps. Right? | No, it's a sensible tool anyone can ask for | Yes, only wimps who can't take a bit of pain use it | Yes, it's only for babies | No, it's only for adults having operations
test: Which question is fine to ask before a blood test? | How long will it take and how will it feel? | None, children aren't allowed to ask questions | Only "can I go home now?" | Only grown-ups ask questions
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the fear of needles. | It is common amongst both children and adults | It is confined to infants and young children | It is indicative of a disorder of the blood | It is unknown amongst adults
exam: Identify the principal reason for which deliberate slow exhalation may diminish the pain of an injection. | It calms the bodily response to fear | It thickens the skin of the arm | It numbs the arm by cooling the blood | It reduces the volume of blood
exam: Whereas a patient has previously fainted during venepuncture, determine the precaution customarily adopted. | The procedure is performed with the patient lying down | The procedure is performed with the patient standing upright | The procedure is abandoned permanently thereafter | Food is withheld for twenty-four hours
exam: Ascertain the most accurate characterisation of bravery in the context of medical treatment. | The undertaking of the treatment notwithstanding fear | The complete absence of fear in the patient | The suppression of all tears | The refusal of numbing preparations
